We've been going in circles. Let me actually reason carefully about the code.

The encrypt function:

```
uint64_t encrypt(uint64_t plaintext) {
    uint32_t left = getleft(plaintext);
    uint32_t right = getright(plaintext);
    right ^= left;
    for (int round_num = 0; round_num < 4; round_num++) {
        uint32_t mixed = f_function(right ^ key[round_num]);
        uint32_t temp = right;
        right = left ^ mixed;
        left = temp;
    }
    left ^= right;
    return merge(right, left);
}
```

So after the pre-round: (L0, R0) = (PL, PR^PL).
Round r: newR = Lr ^ F(Rr^Kr), newL = Rr. So the pair after round r is (Rr, Lr ^ F(Rr^Kr)).

After round 3: (L4, R4) where L4 = R3, R4 = L3 ^ F(R3^K3).
Post: L4 = L4 ^ R4 = R3 ^ L3 ^ F(R3^K3). Then it returns merge(R4, L4) = (R4 high, L4 low).

So ciphertext = (cl, cr) where cl = R4 = L3 ^ F(R3^K3), cr = L4 = R3 ^ L3 ^ F(R3^K3).

Thus cr ^ cl = R3 ^ L3 ^ F ^ L3 ^ F = R3. Oh nice! cr^cl = R3. And cl = L3 ^ F(R3^K3) => F(R3^K3) = cl^cr... wait F = cl ^ L3, and R3 = cl^cr. So we have F(R3 ^ K3) = cl ^ L3, where R3 = cl^cr. That still has L3 unknown.

Hmm, but if we peel back round 3 to get (L3, R3): we have R3 = cl ^ cr (from above) and we need L3. L3 = F(R3^K3) ^ cl. Still needs K3. OK.

But that's exactly what the attack is doing: guess K3, and for the correct guess F(R3^K3) ^ cl must be consistent across all pairs as the "L3" that then feeds round 2. Wait, but the consistency check used in the attack was L[i] != R[i] ^ f(L[i]^K). Let me re-derive what check is correct.

Given guessed K3, for each pair compute candL3 = cl ^ F((cl^cr) ^ K3). Then to check round 2 you need the same candL3 to give a consistent candL2. The attack I've been running instead checks L != R ^ f(L^K) using L and R as the final halves. That's not the right relation, because L (the final left half, which is the post-round L4) is R3^L3^F, not simply L3.

So actually we DO need to peel off the post-round mixing first. Let me write that out: given a pair, define R3 = cl^cr and keep a "mixed" value M = F(R3^K3) = cl ^ L3. We don't know L3, but we do know that M must equal F of a value we know, namely R3^K3. That's already a complete test of K3! For the right K3, the value F(R3^K3) gives a well defined L3 for every pair. But actually that's true for any K3. The real test of K3 has to come from the next round.

So the correct procedure for each round is: for a guess K, define peeled L = cl ^ F((cl^cr)^K) and peeled R = cl^cr. That peeled pair is the input to the previous round. Then the previous round's guess K' must make that input pair fit the same pattern. But what is "the same pattern"? The pattern is just that the pair was produced by the previous round, and that again has a free parameter.

The only way to get an absolute check is to continue peeling back to round 0 and then test that the resulting pair agrees with the known original plaintext after the pre-mixing is put back. That is, the final peeled L and R must satisfy L = PL and R^L = PR. Since we know both PL and PR we have an absolute condition to check against. So the whole thing is nothing more than a four level search in which the last level makes use of that known final condition. It's the same thing that the program for the reverse direction is doing, only that program has the four keys already in its possession. The search for them is to be made by means of the same four steps in reverse order.

That is precisely what has to be put in execution. And it must have failed in the attempts that have been made from the cause of a mistaken view of the nature of the several steps. Now that the whole thing is to be seen in that true light the carrying out of it presents no further difficulty.

